---
import ButtonStudy from "../../../components/studies/ButtonStudy.svelte";
import ButtonTokenAudit from "../../../components/studies/ButtonTokenAudit.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((item) => item.slug === "buttons");
if (!study) throw new Error("Missing buttons study metadata");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section>
		<h2>01 / Situations before styling</h2>
		<p>
			Revision 9 · 28 October 2026 · Purpose and shared-language direction
			clarified by the owner. Study accepted by the owner; integration is the
			next step.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Game-state navigation:</strong>
				startup, pause, continue, setup, and mode selection are screens or modal
				states of the game. Their buttons move the player between states. Start
				game and Continue can be the primary action; navigation is not
				inherently secondary.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Application state actions:</strong>
				players save ship designs, save settings, revert edits, or reset forms.
				These actions operate on application data rather than primarily changing
				the screen. Saving and resetting need clear outcomes and availability
				states.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>The Lab uses the same language:</strong>
				developer tools inherit the application’s button styling and hierarchy.
				Group controls by purpose within their context; do not invent a separate
				visual system for tooling.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Hierarchy follows the player’s immediate goal, not whether an action is
			navigation or persistence. HTML semantics follow behavior: links for URL
			destinations, buttons for in-app state transitions and commands.
		</p>
		<p>
			Observed baseline: <a href="/lab/buttons">current button gallery</a> and
			<a href="/lab/flight">flight trials</a>. The game-state, save, and replay
			examples below are local fixtures; they do not change the game or persist
			data.
		</p>
	</section>
	<ButtonStudy client:load />
	<ButtonTokenAudit />
	<section>
		<h2>05 / Accepted direction</h2>
		<p>
			Use a shared primary, secondary, and destructive hierarchy across game
			screens, player tools, settings, and the Lab. The primary button advances
			the main goal: Start game, Continue game, Save settings, or Save blueprint
			depending on context.
		</p>
		<p>
			Compose navigation as a deliberate group with a clear main path and
			supporting destinations. Compose data actions around a stable save
			location, with revert/reset separated by consequence. Apply those same
			rules to Lab tools.
		</p>
		<p>
			The two compositions are a transparent vertical group with equal-width
			buttons and an anchored action strip with a solid background. Examples now
			use the application’s shared angled-corner button styles and colors.
			Padding now matches the button gap: 8px in both vertical groups and
			strips. Action order remains under review.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Accepted outcome:</strong>
			vertical groups and anchored strips form the shared container vocabulary
			for game screens, forms, and Lab tools.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>06 / Decision record &amp; integration</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Revision 1 explored generic confirmation dialogs, Lab commands, and
				blueprint saving, with anchored and equal-width alternatives.
			</li>
			<li>
				Owner correction, revision 2: “dialog buttons” primarily navigate the
				game’s startup, pause, setup, and mode states. A generic discard
				confirmation was the wrong lead example.
			</li>
			<li>
				Owner direction: application actions include saving player ship designs
				and settings, and resetting forms. Lab tooling follows the same styling
				and hierarchy across the application.
			</li>
			<li>
				Revised examples lead with game-state navigation, then player data
				actions, then Lab reuse. Confirmation remains only a supporting
				consequence example.
			</li>
			<li>
				Revision 3: owner requested the existing angled corners and colors.
				Examples now import shared button styles; layout remains the subject of
				comparison.
			</li>
			<li>
				Retrospective note for the end of this loop: the owner found the
				situation-first explanation useful. Preserve this evidence for review
				before changing workflow guidance.
			</li>
			<li>
				Revision 4: owner defined two containers: transparent vertical groups
				with equal-width buttons, and solid-background horizontal strips using
				anchored groups. These replace the equal-width strip alternative.
				Narrow-strip stacking is a provisional responsive behavior.
			</li>
			<li>
				Revision 5: owner requested equal spacing around and between buttons,
				including transparent containers. Normal vertical spacing maps to the
				existing 16px token; minimal strip spacing maps to 8px.
			</li>
			<li>
				Revision 6: owner found 16px vertical spacing too loose. Reduce vertical
				padding and gaps together to 8px; strip spacing stays at 8px.
			</li>
			<li>
				Revision 7: owner endorsed the baseline and documentation, and requested
				a complete token inventory before promotion. Audit flags hard-coded
				values and component-specific tokens; additions remain proposals.
			</li>
			<li>
				Revision 8: owner established --_name for private component tokens.
				Audit now separates private composition from missing shared foundations;
				the local group-spacing alias adopts the convention without visual
				changes.
			</li>
			<li>
				Future screen-specific work: action order and reset confirmation follow
				each workflow; they do not block this shared baseline.
			</li>
			<li>
				Acceptance: owner approved the completed study and authorized
				integration. Integration: shared tokens, buttons, groups, canonical Lab,
				and existing application screens.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Implemented in <a href="/lab/buttons">the canonical button Lab</a>,
			<a href="/">game startup, pause, and run-complete screens</a>,
			<a href="/lab/flight">flight Lab actions</a>,
			<a href="/settings">settings navigation</a>, and
			<a href="/leaderboard">leaderboard actions</a>. Future ship-designer
			screens should consume the same shared primitives. The audit preserves the
			pre-promotion values and records integration decisions.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
